\title{An Exact Betti--Rank Formula and a Width Calculus for\\
Piecewise-Linear Decision Surfaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The decision surface of a rectified-linear (ReLU) neural network
$f:\mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$, defined as the level set $V(f)=\{x: f(x)=0\}$,
is a piecewise-linear hypersurface: on each activation region the network acts as
an affine map, so $V(f)$ is assembled from flat faces, each cut out by a single
linear equation. Every such face is a hyperplane section---an \emph{algebraic
cycle} of the simplest kind---so the analogue of the Hodge problem (that rational
homology classes are spanned by algebraic cycles) holds for these surfaces
\emph{by construction}. The substantive question is quantitative. We prove an
exact Betti--rank identity: for a three-term chain complex
$C_2 \xrightarrow{d_2} C_1 \xrightarrow{d_1} C_0$ over a field,
$\dim H + \rank d_1 + \rank d_2 = \dim C_1$, equivalently
$\dim H = \dim C_1 - \rank d_1 - \rank d_2$. We then develop a width calculus for
the activation-pattern count $P(w)=\prod_i 2^{w_i}$---closed form, monotonicity,
and multiplicativity under parallel composition---and combine it with the exact
identity to obtain the monotone width bound $\dim H \le \prod_i 2^{w_i}$ on every
Betti number of the decision surface.
\end{abstract}

